WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:03.260
- So the question is, can
Congress impeach president Trump

00:00:03.260 --> 00:00:05.370
after he's already left office?

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:07.020
It's a really interesting question,

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:08.990
because the text of the Impeachment Clause

00:00:08.990 --> 00:00:11.040
of the Constitution seems to imply that

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:13.090
the person still holds an office.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:16.630
After all one of the main
results of an impeachment

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:20.120
can be, although it's not
necessarily, removal from office.

00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:21.650
Remember the impeachment is the indictment

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:24.810
and then the Senate would try
the actual impeachment charges

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:26.400
and potentially remove you from office.

00:00:26.400 --> 00:00:27.980
Then again, another one of the punishments

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:29.840
is not being able to hold office again.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:31.970
So that might be a reason
to try them afterwards,

00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.830
but in terms of historical
precedent in 1876

00:00:34.830 --> 00:00:37.040
the Congress actually did impeach

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.390
the Secretary of War who
had already resigned.

00:00:39.390 --> 00:00:40.810
So there is some past precedent.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:42.850
Anyway, stay tuned for
more election coverage

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:44.643
and I'll see you in court.

